    "**What this cell does:**  \n",
    "Defines the main function of the project — `generate_butterfly_chain()`. It takes any conflict event as plain English input, sends it to Gemma 3, and returns a fully structured cause-effect chain.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Two fixes applied vs the original version:**\n",
    "\n",
    "**Fix 1 — Token limit was too low.** The original used `max_new_tokens=400`. At that limit, Gemma 3 fills up before reaching sections 4, 5, KEY NUMBERS, and HIDDEN RIPPLE — exactly the sections that were cut off. The default is now `900`, with an option to go to `1200` for more detail. Think of `max_new_tokens` like the number of words you allow the AI to write. 400 words isn't enough for 8 structured sections.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Fix 2 — Response extraction was unreliable.** The original split on the word `'model'` to strip out the prompt — but that word can appear inside the answer too (e.g. \"economic model\", \"financial model\"), causing the response to be silently cut in half. The fix slices the output tensor directly: `output_ids[0][prompt_token_count:]` gives us only the answer tokens before any text decoding happens. This is clean, exact, and never accidentally cuts the response.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why `do_sample=True` with `temperature=0.7`?** With `do_sample=False`, the model always picks the single most likely next word — safe but repetitive. With sampling on and temperature at 0.7, it considers a range of likely words and picks from them, producing more natural, varied analysis. Think of 0.0 as a robot reading a script, and 1.0 as freeform improvisation — 0.7 is a confident expert giving a thoughtful answer."
   ]
